#. from yeager:stellarium
msgid "Dante Alighieri mentions several stars and a few constellations: predominantly, the zodiacal constellation of Gemini. The Twins occurs in his texts (in the comedia as well as his poems). As Dante is not an astronomer but a person with broad knowledge, his view of the constellations is highly like not based upon observations of the sky but on manuscripts that he read. Therefore, we chose to take the zodiacal constellation images from Michael Scotus whose manuscript was very popular in Dante's time.\n\nDante Alighieri was a citizen of Florence, Italy, who was later banned and lived at several places in Northern Italy. The comedia was written around 1300 but finished only short before his death in 1321. It is commonly assumed that the story plays in around the Christian festival of Easter in 1300 CE."
msgstr "Dante Alighieri nämner flera stjärnor och några få konstellationer: främst zodiakkonstellationen Gemini. Tvillingarna förekommer i hans texter (både i comedia och i hans dikter). Eftersom Dante inte är astronom utan en person med bred kunskap, är hans syn på stjärnbilderna högst sannolikt inte baserad på observationer av himlen utan på manuskript som han läste. Därför har vi valt att hämta bilderna av zodiakens stjärnbilder från Michael Scotus, vars manuskript var mycket populärt på Dantes tid.\n\nDante Alighieri var en medborgare i Florens, Italien, som senare förvisades och levde på flera platser i norra Italien. Comedia skrevs omkring 1300 men avslutades endast kort före hans död 1321. Det antas allmänt att berättelsen utspelar sig kring den kristna påskhögtiden år 1300."

#. from yeager:stellarium
msgid "### How this Sky Culture was made\n\nThe Dante experts of the German Dante Society selected some text passages with astronomical references in order to present them at Planetarium Jena in the dome. Professor Blume's suggestion that Dante likely had Michael Scotus's drawings of the constellations in mind was accepted. Therefore, we copied these drawings and mapped them onto the stars. The \"16\" bright stars that are mentioned by Dante are usually interpreted by romanists as the stars of first magnitude in the Almagest. Therefore, these stars where highlighted with the \"star\"-symbol in Stellarium. Additionally, we painted a chariot (wagon) and a horn.\n\nDante calls the seven bright stars of Ursa Major \"The Chariot\", the constellation of Ursa Minor \"The Horn\", and possibly refers to Corona Borealis when he mentions a ring of stars. The latter interpretation is in our view unlikely but is discussed among researchers which is why we marked the stars of Corona Borealis as it is described in the Almagest."
msgstr "### Hur denna Sky Culture skapades\n\nDante-experterna i det tyska Dante-sällskapet valde ut några textpassager med astronomiska referenser för att presentera dem på Planetarium Jena i kupolen. Professor Blumes förslag att Dante sannolikt hade Michael Scotus teckningar av stjärnbilderna i åtanke accepterades. Därför kopierade vi dessa teckningar och mappade dem på stjärnorna. De \"16\" ljusstarka stjärnor som Dante nämner brukar av romanister tolkas som stjärnorna av första magnituden i Almagest. Därför markerades dessa stjärnor med \"stjärna\"-symbolen i Stellarium. Dessutom har vi målat en vagn (chariot) och ett horn.\n\nDante kallar de sju ljusstarka stjärnorna i Stora björnen för \"vagnen\", stjärnbilden Lilla björnen för \"hornet\" och hänvisar möjligen till Corona Borealis när han nämner en ring av stjärnor. Den senare tolkningen är enligt vår mening osannolik men diskuteras bland forskare och därför har vi markerat stjärnorna i Corona Borealis så som den beskrivs i Almagest."

#. from yeager:stellarium
msgid "The Italian Dante Society (Societá Dantesca Italiana) provides Dante's work online: [Dante_Online](https://danteonline.it/opere/index.php).\n\n### Fair Use\n\nWe provide this sky culture free of charge, but the authors deserve to be cited according to the usual rules. Thank you!\n\nSusanne M Hoffmann, Dieter Blume, Karl Ph. Ellerbrock, Eduardo Castadura, Bodo Zöll (2022)"
msgstr "Det italienska Dante-sällskapet (Societá Dantesca Italiana) tillhandahåller Dantes verk online: [Dante_Online](https://danteonline.it/opere/index.php).\n\n### Rättvis användning\n\nVi tillhandahåller denna himmelskultur kostnadsfritt, men författarna förtjänar att citeras enligt de vanliga reglerna. Vi tackar dig för din hjälp!\n\nSusanne M Hoffmann, Dieter Blume, Karl Ph. Ellerbrock, Eduardo Castadura, Bodo Zöll (2022)"

#. from yeager:stellarium
msgid "This sky culture was added to Stellarium by Susanne M. Hoffmann (service@uhura-uraniae.com) as a byproduct of a collaboration with Prof. Dr. Dieter Blume (Art History, FSU Jena, Germany), The German Dante Society (Deutsche Dante Gesellschaft) and Planetarium Jena.\n\nWritten in English: Susanne M. Hoffmann.\n\n### Notes\n\nRegion of origin: Florence, Italy; this single sky culture was made for use in research."
msgstr "Denna himmelskultur lades till i Stellarium av Susanne M. Hoffmann (service@uhura-uraniae.com) som en biprodukt av ett samarbete med Prof. Dr. Dieter Blume (konsthistoria, FSU Jena, Tyskland), det tyska Dante-sällskapet (Deutsche Dante Gesellschaft) och Planetarium Jena.\n\nSkriven på engelska: Susanne M. Hoffmann.\n\n### Anteckningar\n\nRegion av ursprung: Florens, Italien; denna enda himmelskultur gjordes för att användas i forskning."

#. from yeager:stellarium
msgid "Greek (Farnese + Almagest)"
msgstr "Grekisk (Farnese + Almagest)"

#. from yeager:stellarium
msgid "This ancient Greek celestial culture is based on the marble globe of the Atlas statue in the Villa Farnese (Naples). The statue dates to the 2nd century AD, i.e. Roman times. However, it is a copy of an older Greek model, which itself has not survived."
msgstr "Den här antika grekiska himmelskulturen är baserad på marmorgloben till Atlasstatyn i Villa Farnese (Neapel). Statyn dateras till 200-talet e.Kr., dvs. romartiden. Den är dock en kopia av en äldre grekisk modell, som i sin tur inte har överlevt."

#. from yeager:stellarium
msgid "The marble globe only depicts figures and no stars. The globe has a diameter of about 65 cm. The figures were not aligned according to exact point coordinates, but in a grid: the ecliptic, the celestial equator, the tropics and the polar circles provide orientation in terms of position in \"latitude circles\" on the celestial sphere, while the coluric circles provide a four-way division in \"longitude circles\". Ancient globe makers used this very coarse-meshed grid to adjust the figure drawings.\n\nThe images of the globe therefore clearly show that the stonemason who created the globe had the popular doctrinal poem by Aratos of Soloi (~300 BCE) in mind. There is no reason for the claim - often inflated by popular science - that this marble globe was carved according to Hipparchus' star catalogue. This thesis has been repeatedly raised over the past ~200 years, but regularly dismissed: Both mathematical arguments and artistic representations speak against it.\n\n<p><img width=\"800\" src=\"illustrations/Atlas_(Farnese_Globe).jpg\"/></p>\n<p><i>Photograph of the reconstructed statue of the Atlas Farnese (Gabriel Seah, CC BY-SA-3.0, 9. Juli 2006, Museo Archeologico Nazionale, Naples).</i></p>\n\nOnly the torso of the man was preserved and with broken elbows: everything below the hip has been supplemented in a modern way. In this form, the sculpture is 2.10 m high. The sculpture of the globe with constellations, which is important for us, is original, but has a large hole at the north pole - presumably it was anchored there to other parts of the architecture. Therefore, the circumpolar constellations of the two she-bears and parts of the dragon are missing.\n\n### How was this Sky Culture made?\n\nThe illustrations of the individual figures were copied from the marble globe as pencil sketches. Stellarium works with point coordinates of the modern HIPPARCOS catalogue (produced by an astrometry satellite), so that the historical drawings have to be locked at three points (stars) each. Since this sometimes leads to distortions in historical figures (the marble globe is not a perfect sphere, the figures were not fitted according to point coordinates), this was done here \"by eye\" - just as by the historical stonemason.\n\nThe names of the constellations and individual stars were taken from the Almagest star catalogue, which also dates from the 2nd century (i.e. the reign of the Roman Emperor Hadrian) and - as was customary at the time - was written in Greek as scientific literature.\n\nThe marble globe was a decorative element in a building, probably a particularly elaborate capital. It has no scientific accuracy. Therefore, some drawings in relief deviate from the exact star positions of the star catalogue: e.g. the Twins (Gemini) are moved slightly to the side to show the figure completely, although the lower part of this celestial region would be covered by the hand of the Atlas statue. There are similar distortions in the Scorpion.\n\nThe following table lists the original Greek names of the constellations in columns 2 and 3, and in column 4 the image on the globe.\n\n<p>\n<table>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><b>Abbreviation</b></td>\n		<td><b>Original Name</b></td>\n		<td><b>Translation</b> (literally)</td>\n		<td><b>Picture</b></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>UMi</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Mu;&iota;&kappa;&rho;&#942; &#702;&#714;&Alpha;&rho;&kappa;&tau;&omicron;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Little She-Bear</td>\n		<td>missing</td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>UMa</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Mu;&epsilon;&gamma;&alpha;&lambda;&#942; &#702;&#714;&Alpha;&rho;&kappa;&tau;&omicron;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Great She-Bear</td>\n		<td>missing</td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Dra</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Delta;&rho;&#940;&kappa;&omega;&nu;</notr></td>\n		<td>Dragon</td>\n		<td>only the head and one bend are depicted<br /><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/dra_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Cep</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Kappa;&eta;&phi;&epsilon;&#973;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>King Kepheus</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/cep_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Boo</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Beta;&omicron;&#974;&tau;&eta;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Ploughing Man</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/boo_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>CrB</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Sigma;&tau;&#941;&phi;&alpha;&nu;&omicron;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Wreath</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/crb_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Her</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&#702;&Epsilon;&nu;&gamma;&#972;&nu;&alpha;&sigma;&iota;&nu;</notr></td>\n		<td>Kneeler</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/her_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Lyr</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Lambda;&#973;&rho;&alpha;</notr></td>\n		<td>Lyre (a Greek instrument)</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/lyr_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Cyg</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&#702;&#714;&Omicron;&rho;&nu;&iota;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Bird [not specified]</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/cyg_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Cas</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Kappa;&alpha;&sigma;&sigma;&iota;&#941;&pi;&epsilon;&iota;&alpha;</notr></td>\n		<td>Queen Kassiepeia</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/cas_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Per</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Pi;&epsilon;&rho;&sigma;&epsilon;&#973;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Perseus</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/per_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Aur</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&#703;&Eta;&nu;&#943;&omicron;&chi;&omicron;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Charioteer</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/aur_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Oph + Ser</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&#702;&Omicron;&phi;&iota;&omicron;&#973;&chi;&omicron;&sigmaf; + &#702;&#714;&Omicron;&phi;&iota;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Snake Carrier, Snake</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/oph_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Sge</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&#702;&Omicron;&iota;&sigma;&tau;&#972;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Arrow</td>\n		<td>missing</td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Aql</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&#702;&Alpha;&epsilon;&tau;&#972;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Eagle</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/aql_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Del</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Delta;&epsilon;&lambda;&phi;&#943;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Dolphin</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/del_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Equ</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Pi;&rho;&omicron;&tau;&omicron;&mu;&#942;</notr></td>\n		<td>Bust [of a horse]</td>\n		<td>missing (stars belong to Dolphin)</td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Peg</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&#703;&#714;&Iota;&pi;&pi;&omicron;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Horse [unnamed]</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/peg_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>And</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&#702;&Alpha;&nu;&delta;&rho;&omicron;&mu;&#941;&delta;&alpha;</notr></td>\n		<td>Princess Andromeda</td>\n		<td><img width=\"355\" src=\"illustrations/and_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Tri</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Tau;&rho;&#943;&gamma;&omega;&nu;&omicron;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Triangle</td>\n		<td>missing</td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Ari</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Kappa;&rho;&iota;&#972;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Ram</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/ari_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Tau</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Tau;&alpha;&upsilon;&#771;&rho;&omicron;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Bull</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/tau_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Gem</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Delta;&#943;&delta;&upsilon;&mu;&omicron;&iota;</notr></td>\n		<td>Twins</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/gem_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Cnc</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Kappa;&alpha;&rho;&kappa;&#943;&nu;&omicron;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Cancer</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/cnc_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Leo</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Lambda;&#941;&omega;&nu;</notr></td>\n		<td>Lion</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/leo_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Vir</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Pi;&alpha;&rho;&theta;&#941;&nu;&omicron;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Young Lady</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/vir_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Lib</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Chi;&eta;&lambda;&alpha;&#943;</notr></td>\n		<td>Claws [of the Scorpion carrying a Balance]</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/lib.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Sco</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Sigma;&kappa;&omicron;&rho;&pi;&#943;&omicron;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Scorpion</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/sco_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Sgr</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Tau;&omicron;&xi;&#972;&tau;&eta;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Archer (two legs: this is satyr Kroton, not the centaur Cheiron)</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/sgr_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Cap</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Alpha;&#943;&gamma;&#972;&kappa;&epsilon;&rho;&omicron;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>the One with Horns of a Goat</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/cap.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Aqr</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&#703;&Upsilon;&delta;&rho;&omicron;&chi;&#972;&omicron;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Water-Pourer</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/aqr_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Psc</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&#702;&Iota;&chi;&theta;&#973;&epsilon;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Two Fishes</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/psc_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Cet</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Kappa;&eta;&#771;&tau;&omicron;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Sea Monster</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/cet_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Ori</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&#702;&Omega;&rho;&#943;&omega;&nu;</notr></td>\n		<td>Orion</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/ori_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Eri</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Pi;&omicron;&tau;&alpha;&mu;&#972;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>River [unnamed]</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/eri.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr>\n		<td><notr>Lep</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Lambda;&alpha;&gamma;&omega;&#771;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Hare</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/lep_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>CMa</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Kappa;&#973;&omega;&nu;</notr></td>\n		<td>Dog</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/cma_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>CMi</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Pi;&rho;&omicron;&kappa;&#973;&omega;&nu;</notr></td>\n		<td>The asterism before the Dog</td>\n		<td>missing [covered by the hand of Atlas]</td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Arg</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&#702;&Alpha;&rho;&gamma;&omega;</notr></td>\n		<td>Ship &quot;Argo&quot;</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/arg_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Hya + Crt + Crv</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&#703;&#714;&Upsilon;&delta;&rho;&omicron;&sigmaf; + &Kappa;&rho;&alpha;&tau;&#942;&rho; + &Kappa;&oacute;&rho;&alpha;&xi;</notr></td>\n		<td>Watersnake + Crater + Raven</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/hya_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Cen</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Kappa;&#941;&nu;&tau;&alpha;&upsilon;&rho;&omicron;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Kentauros</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/cen_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Lup</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Theta;&eta;&rho;&#943;&omicron;&nu;</notr></td>\n		<td>Monster</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/lup_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Ara</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Theta;&upsilon;&mu;&iota;&alpha;&tau;&#942;&rho;&iota;&omicron;&nu;</notr></td>\n		<td>Altar</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/ara_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>CrA</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Sigma;&tau;&#941;&phi;&alpha;&nu;&omicron;&sigmaf; &Nu;&#972;&tau;&iota;&omicron;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Southern Wreath</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/cra_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>PsA</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>&Nu;&#972;&tau;&iota;&omicron;&sigmaf; &#702;&Iota;&chi;&theta;&#973;&sigmaf;</notr></td>\n		<td>Southern Fish</td>\n		<td>missing [covered by the shoulder of Atlas]</td>\n	</tr>\n</table></p>\n\n### Fair Use\n\nWe provide this sky culture free of charge, but the authors deserve to be cited according to the usual rules. Thank you!"
msgstr "Marmorgloben visar endast figurer och inga stjärnor. Globen har en diameter på ca 65 cm. Figurerna är inte utplacerade enligt exakta punktkoordinater, utan i ett rutnät: ekliptikan, himmelsekvatorn, tropikerna och polcirklarna ger orientering i form av position i \"latitudcirklar\" på himmelssfären, medan kolurcirklarna ger en fyrvägsindelning i \"longitudcirklar\". Forntida globetecknare använde detta mycket grovmaskiga rutnät för att justera figurritningarna.\n\nBilderna av jordgloben visar därför tydligt att den stenhuggare som skapade globen hade den populära doktrinära dikten av Aratos från Soloi (~ 300 f.Kr.) i åtanke. Det finns ingen grund för det populärvetenskapligt uppblåsta påståendet att denna marmorglob skulle ha ristats enligt Hipparchos stjärnkatalog. Denna tes har upprepade gånger tagits upp under de senaste ~ 200 åren, men regelbundet avfärdats: Både matematiska argument och konstnärliga framställningar talar emot den.\n\n<p><img width=\"800\" src=\"illustrations/Atlas_(Farnese_Globe).jpg\"/></p>\n<p><i>Fotografi av den rekonstruerade statyn av Atlas Farnese (Gabriel Seah, CC BY-SA-3.0, 9. juli 2006, Museo Archeologico Nazionale, Neapel).</i></p>\n\nEndast mannens torso bevarades och med brutna armbågar: allt under höften har kompletterats på ett modernt sätt. I denna form är skulpturen 2,10 m hög. Den för oss viktiga skulpturen av jordklotet med konstellationer är original, men har ett stort hål vid nordpolen - förmodligen har den förankrats där till andra delar av arkitekturen. Därför saknas de cirkumpolära konstellationerna av de två björnhonorna och delar av draken.\n\n### Hur tillverkades denna himmelskultur?\n\nIllustrationerna av de enskilda figurerna kopierades från marmorgloben som blyertsskisser. Stellarium arbetar med punktkoordinater från den moderna HIPPARCOS-katalogen (som produceras av en astrometrisatellit), så att de historiska teckningarna måste låsas vid tre punkter (stjärnor) vardera. Eftersom detta ibland leder till förvrängningar i historiska figurer (marmorklotet är inte en perfekt sfär, figurerna var inte anpassade enligt punktkoordinater), gjordes detta här \"med ögat\" - precis som av den historiska stenhuggaren.\n\nNamnen på konstellationerna och de enskilda stjärnorna har hämtats från Almagests stjärnkatalog, som även den härstammar från 200-talet (dvs. den romerske kejsaren Hadrianus regeringstid) och - som brukligt var på den tiden - skrevs på grekiska som vetenskaplig litteratur.\n\nMarmorgloben var ett dekorativt element i en byggnad, förmodligen en särskilt genomarbetad huvudstad. Den har ingen vetenskaplig exakthet. Därför avviker vissa teckningar i relief från de exakta stjärnpositionerna i stjärnkatalogen: t.ex. tvillingarna (Gemini) flyttas något åt sidan för att visa figuren helt, även om den nedre delen av denna himmelska region skulle täckas av Atlasstatyns hand. Det finns liknande förvrängningar i Skorpionen.\n\nI följande tabell anges de ursprungliga grekiska namnen på stjärnbilderna i kolumnerna 2 och 3 och i kolumn 4 bilden på jordgloben.\n\n<p>\n<table>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><b>Förkortning</b></td>\n		<td><b>Ursprungligt namn</b></td>\n		<td><b>Översättning</b> (bokstavligen)</td>\n		<td><b>Bild</b></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>UMi</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>Μικρή ʾˊΑρκτος</notr></td>\n		<td>Lilla honbjörn</td>\n		<td>saknad</td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>UMa</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>Μεγαλή ʾˊΑρκτος</notr></td>\n		<td>Stor She-Bear</td>\n		<td>saknad</td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Dra</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>Δράκων</notr></td>\n		<td>Draken</td>\n		<td>endast huvudet och ena böjen är avbildade<br /><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/dra_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Cep</notr></td>\n		<td><notr>Κηφεύς</notr></td>\n		<td>Kung Kepheus</td>\n		<td><img width=\"255\" src=\"illustrations/cep_sw_bg.png\"/></td>\n	</tr>\n	<tr valign=\"top\">\n		<td><notr>Boo</notr></td>\n